      en: "Cho Oyu is the sixth highest mountain in the world, with an altitude of 8,188 meters. It is located in the Himalayas, west of Mount Everest and Lhotse, on the border between Nepal and China. Its name means 'Turquoise Goddess' in Tibetan. The mountain is considered the most straightforward eight-thousander to climb, due to its relatively gentle slopes on the Tibetan northwest side. This makes it a popular preparatory mountain for Mount Everest. The first ascent was made in 1954 by a small Austrian expedition. Despite its accessibility, Cho Oyu poses dangers such as avalanches and the effects of high altitude. The mountain is an important peak for commercial expeditions."
